  "query_preview": "(This evening through Monday)...\nIssued at 227 PM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nSurface analysis this afternoon shows strong high pressure in place \nover Lake Huron, providing easterly surface flow to Central Indiana. \nAloft, strong ridging was in place over the upper midwest, resulting \nin NW flow across Indiana. Water Vapor imagery shows subsidence in \nplace across Indiana and OH. Dew points were in the 50s as mixing \nhas produced lower values. GOES19 shows mostly sunny skies \nacross the area.\n\nTonight and Tuesday...\n\nThe upper ridging over the northern plains states will push east to \nthe eastern Great Lakes tonight and Tuesday, keeping any forcing \ndynamics well north of Central Indiana. The strong surface high \npressure will drift SE to the middle Atlantic states by Tuesday, but \ncontinue to maintain control of our weather. Forecast soundings and \ntime heights show a dry column with subsidence in place through \nTuesday. Dew point depressions tonight may be small, resulting in \npatchy fog, especially in rural areas.\n\nOn Tuesday a mid level inversion appears to remain in place, and \nthis should again prevent much in the way of CU development, \nresulting in another mostly sunny day. Temperatures will be warmer, \nas southerly flow will develop allowing warm air advection. Look for \nHighs to reach the middle and upper 80s\n\nWednesday...\n\nThe upper ridging across the area will have broken down to a \nprogressive, nearly zonal, flow across the northern CONUS by \nWednesday. A short wave within this flow will be pushing across \nsouthern Canada through the day, keeping the best forcing dynamics \nthere. Within the lower levels the tail of an associated cold front \nwill be pushing across the Upper midwest and central Plains. Upper \nforcing along the tail of the front is limited. However, southerly \nflow ahead of the front streaming into Indiana will allow for an \neven warmer and more humid air mass to arrive. Dew points will surge \ninto the 60s. Forecast soundings by late afternoon show diurnal \nconvection possible with marginal CAPE available. Thus will keep \nthings dry through 18Z, but afterward due to diurnal heating and \ninstability, chance pops for showers and storms will be included, \ninto the evening hours. Look for highs in the lower 90s.\n\nThursday...\n\nWith upper flow nearly parallel to a surface front early on \nThursday, chances for rain will continue, especially south. An upper \nimpulse should finally push the front south by early Thursday night. \n\nLocally heavy rain will be a threat with precipitable water values \nover 2 inches and the potential for training storms (with the slow \nmovement of the front).\n\nClouds/rain will keep temperatures cooler with highs around 80 for \nmost areas.\n\nFriday and beyond...\n\nUncertainty rises in this period. The upper ridge to the southwest \nwill try and increase its influence over central Indiana. Meanwhile, \nupper waves moving through the flow to the northeast of the ridge \nwill try to keep the ridge away and bring rain chances to the area. \n\nFor now, believe the best chances for rain will be on Saturday, but \nthat may change. Temperatures will begin to increase again as the \nridge attempts to build northeast, but readings should remain below \n90 for the most part.",
